  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA ridge is expected to build eastward from the Central CONUS \nthrough mid week. The highest 500mb heights are progged to be \nacross the Lower Ohio Valley region on Wednesday. As the ridge \nbuilds eastward, shortwaves are expected to track along the \nnorthern/northeastern periphery of the ridge, crossing the lower\nGreat Lakes/Upper Ohio Valley region. Occasional shower and \nthunderstorm chances are expected as these waves cross, one \ntonight (mainly north of the forecast area,) with another \npossible later Tuesday. There remains uncertainty in the \nplacement and strength of the ridge, and the resulting track of \nany shortwaves on the northern periphery.\n\nThere is a conditional potential for severe storms on Tuesday.\nML CAPE increases to between 2000 and 3000 J/kg by late\nafternoon, with veering in the low levels. An initial warm \nlayer aloft could limit convection, though a possible shortwave \npassage in the afternoon could trigger thunderstorms. This \nscenario remains uncertain due to the reasons mentioned above, \nthough if storms are able to develop severe weather will be \npossible. \n\nThe day with the lowest potential for showers and thunderstorms\nappears to be Wednesday, when the ridge is progged to be the\nstrongest across the Ohio Valley region. Current indications \nare the ridge should begin to break down late Wednesday and \nThursday, as a shortwave and surface cold front approaching. The\npotential for severe storms will also increase by Wednesday \nevening, increasing again on Thursday as convection increases in\ndiurnal heating along the slow moving front. Increasing NW flow\naloft should result in increased shear and diurnal instability.\nThe potential continues for severe storms into Thursday. The \nlatest ML guidance indicates the increasing severe potential, \nespecially late Wednesday and Thursday. Depending on how fast \nthe front drifts south, this potential could continue for at \nleast southern portions of the area into Friday. Dry weather \nshould return by the weekend as the front exits and high \npressure builds in.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nRising 500mb heights with the building ridge should result in\nwarmer temperatures each day through mid week, with the heat\npeaking Wednesday when the ridge is strongest across the Upper\nOhio Valley region. Most locations outside of the higher terrain\nshould reach the 85 to 90 degree range on Tuesday, though there\nis a continued potential for convection and/or patchy cloud\ncover which could affect daytime highs. The heat should peak\nWednesday, as highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s for most \nareas. The combination of heat and humidity should result in \nheat index values in the mid to upper 90s Tuesday and Wednesday.",
